  "problem": "The value $V(t)$ of Fred's plot of land, in thousands of dollars, can be modeled by the function $V(t)=200\\cdot (1.1)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in years since he bought the land. What is the growth in the value of the plot over the first two years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Fred's land grows in value by\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n thousand dollars over the first two years.",
  "worked_solution": "To find the growth in the value over the first two years, we need to find the value after $2$ years and then subtract the initial cost:\n\n$$\nV(2)-V(0)\n$$\n\nIn the previous part, we were given the initial value of the land:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(0) & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $V(2),$ the value of the land after $2$ years:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2) & =200\\cdot (1.1)^{(2)} \\\\  & =200\\cdot (1.21) \\\\  & =242\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the first two years is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2)-V(0) & =242-200 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the value of the land over the first two years was $42$ thousand dollars.",
